+TRACEABILITY (ch:trace) -- the fleet's link graph. Load the skill
+`coding-hermes-traceability` before you file a row, write a test, commit or claim anything.
+Contract: docs/traceability-doctrine.md in the repo that owns the work.
+One recursive grep must find every marker.
+
+MARKER (one line, wherever the claim lives -- board row, code header, test, commit footer, report):
+  ch:trace row=<ID> spec=<path#anchor> wave=<manifest#step> test=<path::test> doc=<path> \
+           prompt=<path> evidence=<artifact-you-produced> witness=<locator-we-cannot-write> \
+           verdict=<id> commit=<sha> memory=<key>
+
+HARD RULES (each one comes from a measured failure, not from taste):
+ 1. row= is MANDATORY. Trace without a work unit is just a comment.
+ 2. NOTHING CLOSES BY REFERENCE. evidence= is an artifact you produced (report, film, log) or a
+    witness the world produced. A marker citing another row, verdict, report or marker is refused:
+    nesting claims is how 222 rows read "complete" with nothing behind them.
+ 3. No witness? Write witness=none:<reason>. A stated reason is acceptable; silence is not.
+ 4. memory= (DuckBrain) never carries a claim alone -- it accompanies a repo-local reference, and
+    only keys that resolve may be cited.
+ 5. verdict= counts only when bound to a commit= reachable from the published tip. A verdict on an
+    unmerged tree is not evidence about shipped code.
+ 6. The checker is never the author. An out-of-band reading outranks anything we wrote.
+
+Rule of thumb: state -> row=, promise -> spec=, claim -> witness=, judgement -> verdict=, history -> memory=.
+Accepted witness forms: tag:<v>@origin | release:<v> | http:<code>+sha256:<digest> |
+path@origin:<path> | ledger:<predicate> | provider:<usage-page> | live:<endpoint>.
+Commit footer: one ch:trace line per row closed, repeated for multi-row work.
+
+BEFORE YOU CLAIM COMPLETION: every claim carries row=; every claim names witness= or
+witness=none:<reason>; nothing closed by reference; any verdict bound to a commit on the published tip.
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+EVIDENCE FLOOR -- what counts as proof, and how far you have to go.
+A claim is only as strong as the layer it was verified at; higher tiers subsume
+the ones below them.
+
+ T0  artifact exists   a file / doc / row was written                  NOT proof
+ T1  compiles, imports go build ./... , python -c "import x"           NOT proof
+ T2  unit / help text  go test ./pkg/... , <cli> --help                NOT proof
+ T3  runs for real     YOU invoked the thing: CLI run, binary executed,
+                       server started, migration applied          <- MINIMUM for "done"
+ T4  live boundary     the real service/DB/mount/file was touched: curl the
+                       running server, query the real store, read the artifact
+                       BACK from where it landed, not from where you wrote it
+ T5  end to end        the user-visible path, entry point through to stored effect
+
+FLOOR: nothing closes below T3 on any path the change touches. T0-T2 alone is this
+fleet's number-one false "done" (import-only / unit-only testing). If you can only
+reach T2, write STATUS: PARTIAL and name the tier you could not reach -- do not close.
+
+Reach T4/T5 whenever the change touches the live path. "Tests pass" is not evidence
+that the RUNNING system changed: name the process, endpoint or store you exercised in
+the same sentence as the claim.
+
+ANTI-PATTERNS (each one measured in this fleet, not hypothetical):
+ - "tests pass" with no command line and no output quoted
+ - reading a stored digest/hash back as proof instead of re-running the command
+ - component-green masking outcome-red (CI green while ~1% of traffic was delivered)
+ - a criterion authored by the same agent that completes it
+ - grepping your own log for a success string instead of probing the effect
+ - closure by reference: naming another row / verdict / report as the evidence
+
+CLOSE-OUT FOOTER (last three lines, exactly this shape -- it is parsed, not read):
+  STATUS: PASS | PARTIAL | FAIL
+  TIER: T<n> -- what you actually ran or touched
+  EVIDENCE: the command, or the artifact path you produced
+STATUS: PASS requires TIER >= T3. PARTIAL names the tier you could not reach.
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+'''
 
